# Design notes — critical decisions

These are the key decisions behind this submission, why I made them, and what I rejected.
Detailed working for each task is in the Word documents in this folder and in `BOM/`.
The full dated log of my decisions is in `Project_Overview_and_Decisions.docx`.

---

## 1. Robot hardware

**Controller — ESP32-WROOM-32 DevKit.**
Same chip as Appendix A. Two CPU cores (one for control, one for Wi-Fi telemetry),
12-bit ADC, PWM on any pin, built-in Wi-Fi. I avoided GPIO 6–11 (wired to the flash chip)
and noted that GPIO 25 is ADC2, not ADC1 as Appendix A states.

**Drive — 2 driven wheels + ball caster (not 4WD).**
A 2WD robot pivots cleanly about its axle for 90° turns. 4WD skid-steer scrubs its tyres in
turns, doubles the motor current and needs four matched motors.

**Motors — N20 6 V 300 RPM metal-gear (not yellow TT).**
I first leaned toward TT motors because they are cheap and easy to mount. I changed to N20
because Meshmerize junctions can be about 30 cm apart, so the robot spends most of its time
braking and turning rather than at top speed. N20s are lighter (about 10 g vs 30 g), have metal
gears with less backlash, and stall at about 1 A instead of 1.5 A, which suits the driver.
Speed with 42 mm wheels: π × 0.042 × 300 / 60 ≈ 0.66 m/s, which is enough.

**Encoders — left out for now (my decision).**
They would allow distance measurement and a proper maze map. I deferred them to keep the
selection-stage robot simple and cheap. They are the first upgrade after selection; encoder N20s
have the same body, and the BOM leaves about ₹1,400 spare.

**Motor driver — TB6612FNG (not L298N), one spare.**
At 1 A the TB6612FNG drops about 0.5 V; the L298N drops 1.8–3.2 V, wasting up to 43% of a
7.4 V battery as heat. The TB6612FNG runs from 3.3 V logic. Its limit is 1.2 A per channel, so I
checked thermals: only a sustained stall is a risk (≈1.3 W per channel vs 0.78 W the package can
shed), which the firmware handles. The L298N would only be better for bigger motors or >13.5 V.

**Line sensor — Pololu QTRX-MD-08RC (not SmartElex RLS-08).**
I asked for "top-notch" sensors and for evidence of how the RLS-08 behaves at 3.3 V. Its maker
rates it for 4.5–5.0 V only; at 3.3 V its LED current would drop to about 55% (estimate), and at
5 V its outputs would need dividers. The QTRX runs from 2.9–5.5 V with a constant 3.5 mA LED
current (same at 3.3 V and 5 V), works best at 10 mm height instead of 3 mm (no risk of
scraping the arena, which disqualifies), and is read on digital pins, so it works with Wi-Fi on.
I considered the 5-channel QTRX-MD-05RC but rejected it: its sensors span only 32 mm, and on a
30 mm line the outer sensors would sit 1 mm from the line edge, ruining junction detection.

**Battery — 2S 1000 mAh 30C LiPo (not 3S, not Li-ion 18650).**
8.4 V when full stays well inside the TB6612FNG's 13.5 V limit (3S at 12.6 V does not).
LiPo over Li-ion: about 57 g vs 110 g, 30 A vs 20 A maximum current, and less voltage sag.
A match uses only about 53 mAh, so the extra capacity of 18650 cells is just extra weight.

## 2. Power design — simplified on purpose

I challenged the first power design and asked for everything non-essential to be removed.
Final design: battery → XT60 → 3 A fuse → switch, then

- straight to the TB6612FNG motor supply, with **one 470 µF capacitor** at its VM pin
  (the only capacitor the datasheet actually requires);
- through an **MP1584 buck converter to 5 V** for the ESP32. I questioned whether it was needed;
  it stays because without it the ESP32's own regulator would turn about 1.2 W into heat.

Removed: the analog multiplexer, the 5 V-rail capacitor, the 0.1 µF capacitors and the battery
voltage divider. All grounds meet at one star point at the battery negative.

## 3. Budget

Total **₹8,605** including shipping and a 15% contingency — **₹1,395 under ₹10,000**.
I dropped the backup RLS-08 sensor and the spare ESP32, but kept a spare motor driver.

## 4. Control (Tasks 1, 3, 9)

- **Task 1 in Python**, so I can explain every line myself.
- **PD control (PID with Ki = 0).** P steers, D damps the swing. The integral would wind up on
  long curves and overshoot corners; it stays in the code with Ki = 0 in case the motors turn out
  mismatched.
- **Proportional motor limiting — my own function.** I replaced the per-wheel clamp with a
  function I wrote that scales both wheel commands by the same factor when either exceeds 255.
  This keeps the left/right ratio, so the robot turns exactly as much as the PID asked for
  (e.g. 260/40 becomes 255/39 instead of 255/40).
- **Task 9 kept deliberately simple:** one file, one function per requirement, Appendix A wiring.
  The only wiring change is IN4 on GPIO 4 instead of GPIO 11 (flash pin).

## 5. Wireless diagnostics (Task 7)

I chose **Wi-Fi** (UDP, ESP32 as its own hotspot). Sending every sample from all 8 sensors at
1 kHz needs about 300 kbit/s, which only Wi-Fi handles comfortably; a glitch from a bump may last
only a few milliseconds and would be missed by down-sampled data. Meshmerize requires wireless
devices to be off near the arena, so this is for the practice track only.

## 6. Maze solving (Task 8)

I chose **DFS (depth-first search)** over the left-hand rule because the Meshmerize sample arena
has 5 loops, and the left-hand rule plus turn-string reduction only finds the shortest path in a
loop-free maze. In my example maze with one loop: left-hand replay 240 cm, DFS map + shortest
route 120 cm. I asked for the code to avoid data-structures topics where possible, so DFS is
written as six if/else rules and the shortest route uses a simple "repeat and improve" loop
instead of Dijkstra's priority queue. Without encoders the robot cannot yet recognise revisited
junctions reliably, so the left-hand rule remains the practical fallback until encoders are added.

## 7. Errors I found in Appendix A and the README

| Item | Problem | Fix used |
|---|---|---|
| IN4 on GPIO 11 | GPIO 6–11 belong to the flash chip | GPIO 4 |
| GPIO 25 "ADC1_CH8" | It is ADC2_CH8 (fails with Wi-Fi on) | Fine in Task 9 (Wi-Fi off) |
| Reduction rule LBR → S | 270° + 180° + 90° = 540° → 180° = B | LBR → B |
| Dark line = high voltage | Meshmerize uses a white line | Invert in `scaleSensors()` for the arena |
